    <p class="corpus-eyebrow">Research methods · exploratory text analysis</p>
    <h1>How to read the transcript analysis</h1>
    <p>This page describes the scoring design, analysis rules, and known limits behind the <a href="/corpus/analysis/">transcript analysis explorer</a>. The ratings are model judgments about language in transcripts. They are not verified facts about the people in a recording.</p>
  </header>

  <article class="methods-copy">
    <section class="methods-brief" aria-labelledby="brief-title">
      <h2 id="brief-title">In brief</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>What is rated.</strong> A language model answers 60 fixed questions about the wording of each transcript passage. Nothing is rated from audio or video.</li>
        <li><strong>Every passage holds 500 words.</strong> How much text a model sees changes its ratings, so all passages are the same length. A recording's score is the mean of its passages.</li>
        <li><strong>Two models.</strong> One model rated every passage. A second model rated every distinct recording again. Their recording scores correlate at about 0.89 for the middle question.</li>
        <li><strong>Eight dimensions.</strong> Related questions are grouped into eight dimensions, fitted on 2,990 distinct recordings. Copies of a recording and texts shorter than one passage keep their question scores but get no dimension scores.</li>
        <li><strong>Videos and streams are kept apart</strong> in every comparison over time, because they differ in length and in what they are about.</li>
        <li><strong>What is not known.</strong> No human has rated these passages yet, so the scores are consistent model readings, not validated measurements. They describe wording, not a person.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is being scored</h2>
      <p>The question bank (version 2) contains 60 questions: 18 about expressed communication style, 10 about expressed emotion, 17 about rhetoric and social acts, three about hypothetical audience reception, nine classifications, two yes-or-no questions about content, and one check of whether the text can be rated at all. The 45 style, emotion and rhetoric ratings are eligible for exploratory factor analysis (EFA). All 48 numeric ratings can appear in correlation and question-map views. Reception estimates stay outside the factor model.</p>
      <p>The unit is the transcript passage, then the recording. A passage can include Daniel, guests, quoted speech, played-back audio or messages read aloud, so these ratings do not isolate one speaker and do not establish anyone's identity, inner state, or the truth of a story. The personality and emotion literature informs the vocabulary, but this is a new, unvalidated instrument, not the BFI-2 or a validated mood scale. See <a href="https://doi.org/10.1037/pspp0000096">Soto and John on the BFI-2</a> and <a href="https://doi.org/10.1037/h0077714">Russell's affect circumplex</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What changed from the first version</h2>
      <p>The first version rated passages of up to 12,000 characters with 78 questions. A re-analysis of its scores showed three problems, and this version was built to remove them.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Passage length drove the scores.</strong> A short final passage was rated one to two and a half standard deviations away from the rest of its own recording, and recordings under 300 words filled the highest and lowest places of every dimension. Passages now all hold 500 words (see below).</li>
        <li><strong>The cohort mixed kinds of recording.</strong> Half-minute clips, ordinary videos and long streams were pooled, and their shares change from year to year, so pooled trends followed the mix. Copies of one recording were counted more than once. Dimensions are now fitted on one version of each recording, and videos and streams are compared apart.</li>
        <li><strong>Parts of the question bank did not do their job.</strong> Six pairs of questions were nearly the same question and were merged. Three features that can be counted (intensifiers, repetition, calls to action) are no longer rated. Eight of eleven reception questions did not go with measured views and were dropped. A subject classification, an addressee classification, a rating of scripted against improvised delivery and two yes-or-no questions were added.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Transcripts are now sent as plain running text without speaker labels or line numbers, so that transcripts of different origin look the same to the model. For the 44 ratings kept from the first version, recording averages of the two versions correlate at 0.91 for the middle question. Levels are not comparable between versions.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Passages, the gate, and recording scores</h2>
      <p>Each transcript is cut into consecutive passages of exactly 500 words, about three minutes of speech. The last passage of a transcript is its last 500 words, so it can overlap the one before it. A transcript of 20 to 499 words is rated once as a short text; it keeps its question scores but takes no part in the dimensions or in trends. A transcript under 20 words is not rated.</p>
      <p>One gate question asks whether the passage is intelligible and holds enough language to rate. If not, the passage's ratings are treated as missing; a clear passage that simply lacks a feature receives zero. The gate is a model judgment, not a human quality check. It passed all but two of 65,577 passages.</p>
      <p>A recording's rating is the plain mean of its passages. The explorer shows how much the passages of a recording differ (their standard deviation). A classification summarizes the passages; it is not automatically a label for the whole video.</p>
      <p>The model receives text, not audio or video. A score is an expected value over five described levels, not a validated psychological unit. The provider documents a probability for each level and a confidence value derived from the same probabilities; the latter is not an independent estimate that the answer is correct. See the <a href="https://docs.typesafe.ai/primitives/score">Score documentation</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why every passage has the same length</h2>
      <p>Before the full run, the same 300 spans of speech were rated at four lengths. Against the 500-word rating, the average intensity question moved by −0.63 standard deviations at 125 words, −0.30 at 250 words and +0.29 at 1,000 words. More text gives a model more occasions to find a feature, whatever the passage is about. Rewording the levels as shares of the passage ("a small part", "about half") did not remove the effect, so the published wording was kept and the length was fixed instead.</p>
      <p>The same shows in the full run: transcripts of 20 to 99 words are rated about one standard deviation lower than full passages on the average question, and those of 100 to 299 words about half a standard deviation lower. This is why short texts carry no dimension scores.</p>
      <p>Other checks from the pilot: two identical requests agree at 0.997 (rank correlation, middle question); sending the questions in four requests instead of one changes nothing; adding speaker labels moves the average question by 0.10 standard deviations.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Two models</h2>
      <p>Every passage was rated by <code>jev-1.14.0</code>, the successor of the model behind the first version; the two versions rank passages alike (0.98). Every distinct recording was rated a second time by a model from another maker, <code>drex-max</code>, on at most 30 evenly spaced passages per recording (33,097 passages of 2,990 recordings). It judged both passages of one video unrateable, so 2,989 recordings have scores from both models.</p>
      <p>The two models rank passages alike but use the scale differently: the second model rates lower on most questions. Scores of the second model are therefore standardized within that model before the dimensions are computed from them. Across recordings, the two models' scores correlate at 0.89 for the middle question (0.70 to 0.96), and at 0.90 to 0.96 for the eight dimensions.</p>
      <p>Agreement between two models shows that a question can be applied consistently. It does not show that either model reads a passage as a person would. A comparison with human ratings of the same passages is planned and has not been done.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Classifications, yes-or-no questions and reception estimates</h2>
      <p>Classifications name a passage's category, subject, addressee, narration mode, presentation, topic focus, text quality, and whether several voices or quoted speech appear. They are model inferences from wording alone and may be wrong; none of them verifies anything about the audio, such as the number of speakers. The two models choose the same subject for 71% of passages.</p>
      <p>The three reception questions estimate how likely a passage is to draw comments, disagreement or divided reactions. They are not surveys and not predictions of views. For 979 videos whose view count was read from archived channel listings about a month after upload, each of the three has a small positive relation with views that are high for their period, channel and length (correlations of about 0.14, with intervals from 0.02 to 0.25).</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Correlations, factor analysis, and the question map</h2>
      <p>Correlations, the question map and the factor model use one version of each recording that has at least one full passage: 2,990 recordings (1,899 videos, 1,086 streams and five clips). Correlation and the multidimensional-scaling (MDS) question map use nonconstant numeric ratings with at least 80% coverage, on one shared complete-case set. Associations may reflect wording overlap, shared model errors, format, or chronology, not causation. Because videos and streams differ on several dimensions, some correlations partly reflect that difference.</p>
      <p>EFA includes only the eligible questions. Each needs at least 80% recording-level coverage, and each included recording must have a usable score for every retained question. The minimum sample is max(100, five recordings per question), 225 for 45 questions. This is a heuristic, not a guarantee of adequacy.</p>
      <p>Horn's PCA parallel analysis suggests the number of factors, eight here; Velicer's MAP suggests ten, and the principal-axis variant more. PCA and common-factor methods answer different questions. Fitted solutions use principal-axis extraction with oblique Promax rotation. They describe covariance in this question set, not "the true dimensions" of a person. See <a href="https://doi.org/10.1037/1082-989X.4.3.272">Fabrigar et al. on EFA practice</a> and <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02289447">Horn's parallel analysis</a>.</p>
      <p>Recording factor scores are regression-estimated coordinates from standardized questions, not 0–4 ratings. Each factor is oriented so that its largest loading is positive: a higher score means more of what the dimension is named after. Higher is not better or worse, and a coordinate is not a measurement of a personal trait.</p>
      <p>The same eight factors reappear when the model is fitted in other ways (Tucker congruence with the published loadings, lowest to highest factor): videos alone 0.94 to 0.99, streams alone 0.79 to 0.95, single passages instead of recording means 0.93 to 0.99, and the second model's ratings 0.86 to 0.94. All 500 refits on resampled months converged.</p>
      <p>The MDS map uses signed chord distance, √(2 × (1 − r)): positive correlations place questions nearer and negative correlations farther apart. Its normalized RMS reconstruction error describes how well a map with a chosen number of dimensions preserves those distances. These are geometric fit diagnostics, not confidence in a score or factor.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Videos, streams and trends</h2>
      <p>A recording of 40 minutes or more, or one filed as a livestream, counts as a stream; this is a rule, not a platform record. Videos have a median of about 1,600 words and streams about 18,500. At equal passage length the two kinds are worded differently: streams score about one standard deviation lower on direct, organized argument and on narrative, and about one higher on energetic and angry wording. A pooled line over time would therefore follow the share of streams, which grows from almost none before 2019 to most recordings in 2025.</p>
      <p>The explorer draws videos and streams as separate lines. Each point is the mean of the recordings of that kind in the period, with a 95% interval for that mean that treats recordings as independent; periods with fewer than five recordings are drawn hollow and without an interval.</p>
      <p>The table under the chart gives the direction of each dimension from 2015 to 2025 four times: for videos and for streams, and from each model. A direction is given when the 95% interval of the yearly slope excludes zero (whole months resampled, 1,000 draws). A trend is said to hold only if all four agree. By that rule, two of the eight dimensions have a trend: "Self-disclosure and self-criticism" falls and "Care and regard for others" falls. The other six have no trend on which all four agree.</p>
      <p>Dates in this table are those of the earliest version of a recording, including estimated dates; the chart uses catalog dates only. Subject is a model classification, and a trend can be a change of subject. When the subjects of each recording are held constant, the yearly fall of self-disclosure in videos shrinks from 0.17 to 0.04 standard deviations and the fall of care from 0.19 to 0.06; in streams the fall of self-disclosure halves and the fall of care disappears. Between 2017 and 2025 videos moved from personal subjects to products: passages about games and products were 2% to 4% of video passages in 2017 and 2018 and 67% to 78% in 2024 and 2025.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Robustness checks and observational associations</h2>
      <p>The offline supplement compares neighboring factor counts on the same complete-case sample and aligns each alternative to the primary solution. The current fitted counts, sample sizes and diagnostics appear in the explorer. Same-count congruence describes loading similarity; cross-count absolute loading cosine is a separate matching statistic. Neither establishes that factors are equivalent or validates the chosen count. Rank-correlation, higher-text-quality, leave-one-major-genre-out and candidate-deduplicated fits test sensitivity to analysis choices. Their agreement is conditional on this instrument and sample; quality and genre groups come from fallible text-model labels.</p>
      <p>The lexical near-duplicate screen uses normalized five-word shingles, a fixed-seed MinHash candidate screen and exact comparison of retained shingle hashes. It forms connected components for an aggressive one-representative-per-component refit; the explorer reports the eligible cohort and candidate counts. Candidates are not confirmed duplicates: quotations, shared scripts, transcription artifacts and transitive chains can link different material. The screen is not exhaustive. It does not compare audio or determine narrative equivalence.</p>
      <p>Length, date and genre associations use factor scores as outcomes. Adjusted ordinary least-squares estimates use only dated complete records and control for log transcript length, recording year and genre indicators, pooling sparse categories. They describe associations within this archive and depend on uncertain dates and model-assigned genres. No p-values or causal interpretations are reported.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Recording map and similar recordings</h2>
      <p>The recording map uses principal components analysis of centered and sample-standardized factor scores of the 2,990 distinct recordings. The explorer reports the variation retained by its displayed axes; omitted variation can make recordings look closer or farther apart than their full profiles suggest. Points can be colored by year, category or kind. These axes differ from the MDS question map, which places questions rather than recordings.</p>
      <p>UMAP is a secondary view of the same recordings and standardized factor coordinates. It uses Euclidean distance, 30 neighbors, minimum distance 0.2, two output dimensions and fixed seed 20260930, computed offline with one thread. Its exact package version and settings are retained with the artifact. See the official <a href="https://umap-learn.readthedocs.io/en/latest/parameters.html">UMAP parameter guide</a> and <a href="https://umap-learn.readthedocs.io/en/latest/reproducibility.html">reproducibility documentation</a>.</p>
      <p>UMAP emphasizes local neighborhoods. Its axes are arbitrary, it has no explained-variance percentage, and visual gaps, cluster sizes and long-range distances should not be read as calibrated differences. The fixed seed makes this saved computation reproducible; it does not establish stability across other seeds or parameter choices. Apparent groups are not verified categories or personality types.</p>
      <p>Similar-recording rankings use Euclidean distance across all standardized factor coordinates. A nearby recording has a similar model-rated profile under this rule; proximity does not establish a shared topic, speaker, event or duplicate source. Other versions of a recording and short texts have no place on the map; the panel says which recording represents them.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Variation within recordings and model ambiguity</h2>
      <p>The passage rankings require at least three usable passages for the selected question. They summarize the spread of passage scores, their minimum and maximum, the last-minus-first score, and the average absolute change between consecutive passages. For each question, the 500 recordings with the largest spread are listed. A large change can reflect a change of topic or of voice; it does not by itself establish an emotional transition.</p>
      <p>One passage says little about a long recording: in streams, only about 10% of a question's variation lies between recordings, the rest between the passages of a recording (50% in videos, whose passages are more alike). Recording averages are more stable than single passages for that reason.</p>
      <p>Entropy is normalized to 0–1 across the five levels: zero indicates all probability on one level and one indicates a uniform distribution. It describes ambiguity in the returned model distribution, while between-passage variance describes differences in passage scores. Neither is a correctness estimate. Coverage tables count passages rejected by the gate and passages missing a numeric value.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Residual structure and held-out reconstruction</h2>
      <p>The residual view subtracts the selected factor model's fitted question correlations from the observed correlations. Large residual pairs identify relationships that this representation leaves unexplained; they can also reflect related wording of two questions or shared model errors.</p>
      <p>The factor-count comparison uses three chronological folds with expanding training periods. Known lexical overlap groups spanning a split are removed from both sides. Training data determine means, scales, loadings and uniquenesses. Each question in a later recording is then predicted using its other 44 ratings, with that target value masked. This evaluates reconstruction of model ratings within a recording, rather than forecasting future audience response.</p>
      <p>The explorer reports each fold's training and test counts, any overlap exclusions, numerical fit status, reconstruction errors and training-mean baseline. A larger representation can improve reconstruction without establishing that it is the true factor count. The 45 questions and the cohort were selected before this comparison and are held fixed.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Adjusted time comparisons and candidate shifts</h2>
      <p>These comparisons are computed for videos and for streams apart; the panel has a selector for the kind. Raw yearly summaries show recording-level distributions. Standardized yearly means use the same genre mix across periods: the dated cohort's mix, after pooling genres below the larger of 30 recordings or 2% of that cohort into "other". Every target stratum must contain at least five recordings in a year. Unsupported standardized means and contrasts remain unavailable; missing strata are not silently dropped or interpolated.</p>
      <p>Calendar calculations include only recordings with a resolvable month; undated recordings remain available in overall analyses but receive no guessed calendar position.</p>
      <p>Candidate shifts use monthly factor means only when a month has at least ten recordings. Low-count months and calendar gaps divide the sequence into separate runs. Penalized segmentation compares minimum segment lengths of three and six months, two penalty settings, and a sensitivity using nonoverlapping two-month averages. Adjacent-month dependence can remain, so agreement between settings is descriptive rather than a significance test. Model-assigned genre, uncertain dates, missing videos and changing formats can influence a shift. No event is attached to a boundary.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the scores and the wording predict</h2>
      <p>Later checks asked whether these measures go with anything outside themselves. Each check held data out, and each result is an association within this archive, not an effect of how someone speaks.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Views.</strong> For 979 videos whose view count was read from archived channel listings about a month after upload, the period, channel, age at the reading, format and length explain about three quarters of the variation in views. The eight dimensions predict none of what is left in held-out calendar quarters. Single questions have small relations: videos rated higher on divided reactions, disappointment, expected comments and disagreement have somewhat more views (correlations of about 0.14 to 0.15), and videos with a more positive tone somewhat fewer. The subject of a video within a period is not controlled, so this describes kinds of video.</li>
        <li><strong>Dates.</strong> The wording of a transcript alone places a dated recording within about three months of its catalog date at the median, and within a year for 83% of recordings. This is used only to estimate dates for recordings with no better evidence; see "How recordings are dated" under Provenance on any recording page.</li>
        <li><strong>Transcript source.</strong> For 48 uploads that have both a cloud transcript and imported subtitles, the two transcripts of the same upload give nearly the same scores: the eight dimensions correlate at 0.98 to 0.99 and differ by less than a tenth of a standard deviation. In the first version, speaker labels and layout made the source matter more.</li>
        <li><strong>Transcript noise.</strong> Two machine transcripts of the same audio disagree on about 1.5% of words for single-voice recordings and about 6% where several people speak. These are lower bounds, because both transcripts can share an error. Word counts and counts of numbers are stable to within a few percent, punctuation-based measures carry an error of 4% to 6%, and exclamation marks and filler words are not comparable between transcript sources.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Several things were looked for and not found: sharp eras in the subjects mentioned, a calendar season for any subject other than holidays, and a drift in the dates that spoken statements such as "three years ago" imply as the years pass.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Three different kinds of uncertainty</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spread across passages:</strong> how much the passages of one recording differ on a question (their standard deviation, shown as ± in the table). It can reflect real changes in topic or voice as well as noise in the ratings. It is not an interval for the recording's mean.</li>
        <li><strong>Range when the model is refitted:</strong> for a dimension score, the 2.5th to 97.5th percentile of that recording's score across 500 refits of the factor model on resampled months. It describes how much the score depends on the fitted model, given this cohort and question bank. It is not a sampling interval and not an interval for a true trait.</li>
        <li><strong>Interval of a period mean:</strong> in the trend chart, a 95% interval for the mean of the recordings of one kind in one period. It treats recordings as independent, which they are not entirely (several recordings of one week share a subject), so it is somewhat too narrow.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>None of these says whether a rating is right. The model's own probabilities can be confidently wrong, and no human-labeled calibration set exists for this instrument. For context on calibration, see <a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/guo17a.html">Guo et al.</a>; calibration methods require independent reference labels.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What a source link establishes</h2>
      <p>Each analysis is tied to a transcript revision and links to its passages by start time. Question timelines place points at passage start times and show vertical bars over the levels that hold the middle 90% of the model's probability; the bars are not confidence intervals. Selecting a point opens that timestamp. A source link establishes which text was processed and where it occurs, not which words caused a rating. A passage starts and ends at a word count, so its first and last transcript lines can belong partly to the neighboring passage. ASR timestamps and wording may be wrong. The <a href="https://docs.typesafe.ai/cookbooks/semantic_find">Typesafe semantic-find example</a> illustrates candidate selection with an explicit no-evidence option.</p>
      <p>Recordings without dimension scores remain browseable with their question scores. Source revisions, not only corpus compatibility, determine whether scores reflect the latest text. Human validation remains unavailable.</p>
    </section>
